Re: Thicker Computer Modern fonts? "[NAME]"   == [NAME]     <|EMAIL|> writes:
"[NAME]" == [NAME] <|EMAIL|> writes:

[NAME]> I'm aware that the CM fonts are supposed to look that way
[NAME]> (light) so I was wondering if there's any workaround to make the
[NAME]> output on paper look thicker/darker. 

[NAME]> In some cases they have commissioned special Type 1 versions
[NAME]> of the CM fonts (or a subset thereof) that are "darker".

[NAME]> When using bitmapped versions of the fonts, people have
[NAME]> modified the MetaFont commands to "fatten" the strokes.

What about just makeing a modes.mf entry with a larger darker than
that used by default for the given printer?  Ie, change ljet4 from:

mode_def ljfour =  %\\[ [PRODUCT] ([PRODUCT])
  mode_param (pixels_per_inch, 600);
  mode_param (blacker, .25);
  mode_param (fillin, 0);
  mode_param (o_correction, 1);
  mode_common_setup_;
enddef;

to something like:

mode_def blackerljfour =  %\\[ [PRODUCT] ([PRODUCT]) with extra blacker
  mode_param (pixels_per_inch, 600);
  mode_param (blacker, 1);
  mode_param (fillin, 0);
  mode_param (o_correction, 1);
  mode_common_setup_;
enddef;

Perhaps also the printer you are using and the [PRODUCT] mode do not
concur.  There are fifteen [PRODUCT] modes in modes.mf, you might prefer
one of the others to whichever one your setup defaults to.

[NAME]

